Oscar-winning documentarian Laura Poitras is acutely aware of the importance of the weight of words when describing issues of great sensitivity. Her work has dealt with Edward Snowden&#x2019;s NSA whistleblowing, the Sackler family&#x2019;s role in the opioid crisis, and Iraq under U.S. occupation, among other burning sociopolitical issues of the 21st century. With &#x201C; They&#x2019;re Here ,&#x201D; the lauded director turns her lens on the U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories ‘Green Room’ Director Remembers Patrick Stewart as a ‘F—ing Sweetheart’ and Credits Him for Rescuing the Film: He ‘Swooped Down’ ‘Into Our Cruddy Ass Movie and Saved Us’ ‘Avengers: Doomsday’ Including the Original X-Men ‘Just Felt Right,’ Says Kevin Feige: It's a 'Fun Way to Culminate' 25 Years

Co-directed with Rachel Lauren Muller, &#x201C;They&#x2019;re Here&#x201D; combines footage captured by the filmmakers with material provided by activists on the ground in Minneapolis during violent ICE raids in the aftermath of the killing of Alex Pretti and Ren&#xE9;e Good. In the short film, images of a victim barricading himself in the trunk of a car to get to work give way to a volunteer resistance organization tracking ICE raids through a citizen-fed counter-surveillance software system. In the film, words like "war zone" are used to describe what it felt like to be in Minneapolis in January of this year.

Speaking with Variety out of IDFA , Poitras says language has been &#x201C;really important in all of my work.&#x201D; &#x201C;We have to reject the euphemisms of the government to describe things. This was a paramilitary occupation of an American city that created an effective war zone.&#x201D;

&#x201C;My co-director, Rachel Lauren Mueller, lives in the neighborhood that we filmed, so she can talk about the fact that there are helicopters hovering, tanks and people being executed in broad daylight,&#x201D; she adds. &#x201C;We need to use the word execution, use the word abduction, kidnapping, occupation, paramilitary force. Because those are the actual English words that describe what&#x2019;s happening. When you use words like deportation, you&#x2019;re capitulating to the government's narrative, which I don't think we should ever do.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Poitras was also interested in the idea of citizens using their phones and available technology at hand to counter-surveil ICE, which she believes to be a &#x201C;very effective form of resistance.&#x201D; &#x201C;State powers need to know that they're being watched, that they can't do this violence in secrecy with total impunity. There will be accountability. There has to be accountability.&#x201D; Citizens, the filmmaker says, know &#x201C;that this is a paramilitary occupation of the United States happening with absolutely no accountability,&#x201D; and their footage is a way to &#x201C;have a record that can be used both as a deterrent and as evidence and to tell the truth.&#x201D;

&#x201C;As a political statement, it's important to say that the master's tools will never dismantle the master's house,&#x201D; she adds. &#x201C;There's only so much we can do. But there is something that's powerful about documenting and counter-surveilling what the state is doing and making sure that they know that they're also being watched and make them nervous about that.&#x201D;

&#x201C;They&#x2019;re Here&#x201D; is permeated by a sustained sense of dread, borrowing from genre to amplify both the loud, anxiety-inducing frenzy of the raids and the dystopian images of lined-up people slowly marched into freight planes from the same airport where thousands of Americans jet off to holidays. The visual language was inspired by films like Alfonso Cuar&#xF3;n&#x2019;s &#x201C;Children of Men&#x201D; and RaMell Ross&#x2019; &#x201C;Nickel Boys.&#x201D;

&#x201C;We very much wanted to use the tools of cinema to capture the sense of horror that was unfolding and to create that uncanny distance of a reality that is unfolding as we speak,&#x201D; she says. &#x201C;We're seeing so much violence, and we don&#x2019;t want to repeat it. How can we denormalize it and put the audience in a perspective of both fear and terror, but also a critical distance where you understand how profoundly abnormal what&#x2019;s happening in the United States is right now?&#x201D;

When asked if witnessing the citizen-led efforts in Minneapolis fed a sense of hope, Poitras says she doesn&#x2019;t know if &#x201C;hope is the right word given the state of the world,&#x201D; but that she believes resistance &#x201C;does have an impact.&#x201D;

Addressing the Democrats' recent success in the U.S. midterm elections, she note, &#x201C;These victories of the progressive movement in the U.S. didn&#x2019;t happen overnight because of some charismatic people. Yes, of course, there are great candidates, but it was years and decades of work laying the foundation without visible results and with many defeats. When you see these successes, you realize, ok, this is a moment. There are tipping points and tables are turned and nobody can surrender our democracies.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

On top of presenting &#x201C;They&#x2019;re Here&#x201D; at IDFA and being on the festival jury, Poitras hosted an awards screening and Q&A for Lance Oppenheim and Nathan Fielder&#x2019;s doc-phenomenon &#x201C;You Can See Everything&#x201D; while on the ground at the Dutch festival.

&#x201C;It&#x2019;s totally, 100% a documentary,&#x201D; she says, quickly rebuffing any idea that the majorly successful film could be anything but. &#x201C;It&#x2019;s dealing with ethical questions of documentary and lifting the fourth wall. Plus, it&#x2019;s fantastic drama. There&#x2019;s this chess match, this psychological thriller between Nathan Fielder, Elizabeth Holmes and Billy Evans that is just fascinating.&#x201D;

The director pointedly says she wants to &#x201C;reject any ideas that documentary filmmakers are going to reject this film because everybody loves to talk about the film.&#x201D; &#x201C;Everybody is so excited about both what it is doing in terms of the genre and pushing boundaries, and also it's a fantastic success. There is also love for both of these filmmakers.&#x201D;

&#x201C;What&#x2019;s so beautiful to watch is that it&#x2019;s not a film that&#x2019;s going to give you answers,&#x201D; she continues. &#x201C;It will provoke endless questions instead, and that is such a fantastic story device. I also think it makes us all think about everything we look at anytime we see reality on camera. And what is reality?&#x201D;

Poitras believes the film is going to be &#x201C;taught in every film school because of how much it teaches us about documentary filmmaking.&#x201D; She is also &#x201C;thrilled&#x201D; for Oppenheim and Fielder&#x2019;s success and the film&#x2019;s gigantic box office, which looks en route to surpassing $100 million globally before the end of the month. &#x201C;We consider them part of our community, not other. To have this success is just great.&#x201D;

&#x201C;I think it&#x2019;s a lock for a Best Picture nomination, and it deserves it,&#x201D; she concludes.

Half a century separates the height of the Harlem Renaissance movement from when lauded filmmaker William Greaves filmed some of its greatest luminaries at Duke Ellington&#x2019;s apartment in 1972. A further half century passed before the footage met audiences through efforts by the filmmaker&#x2019;s son, David, and granddaughter, Liani Greaves, the director and producer of &#x201C; Once Upon a Time in Harlem .&#x201D; The doc rounds off a majorly successful festival run that started at Sundance by landing at IDFA , with the festival also dedicating a retrospective strand to William Greaves&#x2019; work. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Abdul El-Sayed Mocks Trump and Ted Cruz's Cruel Rhetoric on 'The View,' Jokes He Wants 'Jihadist Leprechaun' Printed on a Hat ‘Green Room’ Director Remembers Patrick Stewart as a ‘F—ing Sweetheart’ and Credits Him for Rescuing the Film: He ‘Swooped Down’ ‘Into Our Cruddy Ass Movie and Saved Us’

William Greaves, whose centenary is celebrated in 2026, was born during the flowering of African American art, culture and politics brought by the Harlem Renaissance in the 1920s and 1930s. The filmmaker spent decades trying to turn the footage he shot at the gathering into a film.

Soon after he died in 2014, William&#x2019;s late wife and producer Louise Archambault Greaves worked tirelessly alongside David and Liani to bring the film to life. The resulting doc features legends such as Leigh Whipper, Ida Mae Cullem, Richard B. Moore, Louise Patterson and Richard Bruce Nugent reminiscing about the movement and its legacy. The film opened in the U.S. in October courtesy of Neon .

Speaking with Variety out of the Dutch festival, Liani Greaves says she is &#x201C;moved&#x201D; by how the film has been lovingly received, with the father-daughter duo especially surprised by how it is resonating with younger audiences. &#x201C;It&#x2019;s funny because you would think that it would be mostly older people who would be engaged with the film, but we are finding young audiences are really fascinated by it,&#x201D; she says. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;Neon is doing a wonderful job reaching out to them, whether it&#x2019;s on social media or being proactive in getting schools involved in seeing it,&#x201D; she adds. &#x201C;They&#x2019;ve been a fantastic partner in that. They are specialized in boutique films like ours, so it felt like the perfect partner. They are making sure to hit the arthouses as well as the big theaters. They&#x2019;ve got a wonderful strategy.&#x201D;

&#x201C;Young people are looking for something more real than what is on their phone screens,&#x201D; David notes. &#x201C;They want more information, like with &#x2018;NAZA&#x2019; and &#x2018;American Doctor.&#x2019; They want to find something that is telling them some kind of truth. Young folks have been very much attracted to the film, telling us they did not know this kind of film existed, this kind of people existed.&#x201D;

&#x201C;I was also talking to someone who is in the Palestinian struggle, and I said the poem &#x2018;If I Must Die&#x2019; can be immediately understood in terms of the West Bank,&#x201D; adds David. &#x201C;There&#x2019;s this aspect to it too. Oppressed people, wherever they are, have the same struggle. It&#x2019;s a universal struggle, and that resonates as well.&#x201D;

Liani appreciates how the timeliness of &#x201C;Once Upon a Time in Harlem&#x201D; comes through how it &#x201C;teaches a way to be in order to respond to what we&#x2019;re seeing in the world.&#x201D;

&#x201C;What we&#x2019;re seeing right now is everybody fighting with each other, and that doesn&#x2019;t get us where we need to be. We see the impact of the Harlem Renaissance on a global level because they were in these artistic, cultural, intellectual forums where they could disagree but use that curiosity to build something better.&#x201D;

&#x201C;It was a time when people were having civil discourse,&#x201D; she goes on. &#x201C;George Schuyler says that every ethnic group in Harlem at that time would have intellectual centers in which they would talk about the sundry issues of the day. I don&#x2019;t know that we have that today, but wouldn&#x2019;t that be nice?&#x201D;

When asked if she believes cinemas could become these spaces, the producer says &#x201C;absolutely.&#x201D; &#x201C;We could have made a choice to have this film go to streaming directly, but we wanted it to be in theaters. We wanted people to be in rooms together experiencing the film, knowing that it would generate this conversation and community.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Liani and David used AI technology for &#x201C;90 seconds or so&#x201D; of the film to emulate William Greaves&#x2019; handwriting. When asked about the choice, Liani says the process started from a wish to bring some of her grandfather&#x2019;s personal writing into the film.

&#x201C;We could have had an actor; we could have had my dad do it if we wanted to,&#x201D; she says. &#x201C;But my grandfather always thought of the camera as a sort of apparatus that could be a magnifier that would enliven people in a different way when he saw through that. He used that tool, and he used all of the tools that were available to him at the time, various technology and whatnot. I think he might have even used this one.&#x201D;

The producer notes how she and her father see the film as a &#x201C;collaboration across time and different generations.&#x201D; &#x201C;We use this technology as a container to hold his own words. It&#x2019;s not to make him alive again, but it&#x2019;s also kind of like he&#x2019;s in the universe in a way.&#x201D;

&#x201C;It feels very natural to hear his voice and to see his handwriting,&#x201D; adds David, with Liani mentioning how she feels her grandfather is &#x201C;in the cipher.&#x201D; &#x201C;It feels very much like this was the technology that would be useful in order to have his words be used in the film. It was the choice that we made, that we spent a lot of time on. Again, it&#x2019;s less than 90 seconds. We thought a lot about it, and I feel like we made the absolute best choice for this film.&#x201D;

