For costume designers Janie Bryant and John Glaser, outfitting a character is another form of storytelling.

&#x201C;When an actor appears on screen, you see the costume before they even say a word," says Bryant, an Emmy winner whose credits include "Mad Men," "Deadwood" and Taylor Sheridan shows including "Dutton Ranch" and other "Yellowstone" spinoffs. "That&#x2019;s the magic of costume design." Related Stories 'Chasing Summer' Director Josephine Decker Says That ‘Filmmaking Is Lonelier Than Ever,’ So It’s Time to Build a Community Tony Award Winner Lois Smith Joins Colin West’s ‘Outside of Cleveland’: ‘She’s an Icon’ (EXCLUSIVE)

Bryant and Glaser, an Emmy winner who has worked on "Bridgerton," "Gotham" and "Harry Potter 20th Anniversary: Return to Hogwarts," joined Variety&#x2019;s senior film reporter Rebecca Rubin in partnership with the Round Top Film Festival for a panel titled, "Who, What, Wear: The Importance of Costume in Storytelling." blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

For both, their work starts with the script.

&#x201C;It's kind of our bible,&#x201D; Bryant said. &#x201C;We can see the period. We can read how these characters are speaking to one another, the mood, the feeling. The script is everything.&#x201D;

Adds Glaser, &#x201C;The script is our bible, but you have to dissect it.&#x201D;

Once they have a sense of the characters, they can start to build out their looks. That involves extensive research, especially if they are curating a wardrobe from decades (or centuries) ago, rather than a modern story. In some instances, they are laboring over individually tailored items, dyeing pieces to achieve the perfect shade or sourcing the perfect item. Each costume requires multiple fittings on the actor, as well as creating duplicates for stunt doubles.

